Free will is a fundamental concept in ethics and law, which assumes individuals are responsible for their choices.
Neuroscientific research indicates that the brain can generate spontaneous decisions without being fully determined by prior stimuli.
The existence of moral responsibility implies that people have the capacity to make choices independent of external factors.
In everyday life, people regularly make decisions that contradict previous intentions, showing flexibility in choice-making.
Philosophical traditions, such as existentialism, emphasize human agency and the ability to shape one's own destiny.